From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 06:05:28 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: correct the uncached-window comments

Two comment blocks in this board file state the old, wrong bound on the
uncached alias as measured fact: the memory node's list of limits that
survive the 512 MiB width, and the WARNING on the reserved regions.

Both said the alias ends at PHYS_OFFSET + 0x12310000. It ends at
PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M. That address is the CPC -- the first populated
block in the register space rather than the start of it -- and bounding
at it admitted 35 MiB of physical addresses whose alias faults on the
unpopulated holes and reaches live registers elsewhere. The comments now
describe the window itself: VA 0xA0000000-0xBFFFFFFF is mapped uncached
onto physical 0x20000000-0x3FFFFFFF, of which only the lower half is
DRAM.

The rule both blocks exist to state is unchanged and still holds. Every
region reserved here and in seale_avm.dtsi is below 0x30000000 -- though
only just, since the bootcore window ends exactly there, which the
WARNING now says so that nobody grows one of them upwards.

What the comment does not claim is how ordinary allocations are kept
inside the window, because at this commit nothing does. It names the
memory node's width as the lever, which the next commit pulls.

The alternative -- sizing ZONE_DMA to the window and having dma-direct
allocate coherent memory from it -- is parked rather than taken. It
moved ordinary GFP_KERNEL allocations above 0x30000000 and exposed a
latent linear-alias defect in module function pointers, which blocked
boot; and it silently changed the FRITZ!Box 7560's zone layout, turning
that board's entire memory into ZONE_DMA, which is an unvalidated change
to the one board this platform is proven on.

A third statement in the same block had gone stale earlier and is
corrected with them: the shm RNG seed at 0x3dfb0000 is described as
being out of range of the memory node, which stopped being true when the
node widened to 512 MiB. It is inside the node and above the bound; what
makes it safe is that it is no-map and nothing here maps it.

Comments only. The built DTB is byte-for-byte identical.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index ce4799246b02..69588093bfe1 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -52,13 +52,23 @@ avm-hw-revision {
 	 *
 	 * Two bounds that did NOT move and still apply at 512 MiB:
 	 *
-	 * The uncached KSEG1 alias of DRAM ends at PHYS_OFFSET + 0x12310000 =
-	 * 0x32310000 (~291 MiB), measured. Cached RAM is fine across the
-	 * whole node -- under EVA's SegCtl, KSEG0 maps 0x80000000 to
-	 * 0x20000000 and covers all 512 MiB -- but general
-	 * dma_alloc_coherent() consumers must stay below 0x32310000. hdma is
-	 * already re-bound to that aperture; a new uncached consumer is what
-	 * would break here first, not the node width. The CBM pools at
+	 * The uncached alias of DRAM covers the first 256 MiB only, and stops
+	 * at PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M = 0x30000000. The SegCtl maps kernel VA
+	 * 0xA0000000-0xBFFFFFFF uncached onto physical 0x20000000-0x3FFFFFFF,
+	 * and only the lower half of that is DRAM; the upper half is the
+	 * SoC's register space. Cached RAM is fine across the whole node --
+	 * KSEG0 maps 0x80000000 to 0x20000000 and covers all 512 MiB -- but
+	 * anything taking an uncached alias must stay below 0x30000000. hdma,
+	 * which aliases addresses by hand, carries the same bound itself; for
+	 * every other consumer it is the width of this memory node that has
+	 * to keep allocations inside the window, since an ordinary GFP_KERNEL
+	 * page can otherwise come from anywhere in it.
+	 *
+	 * An earlier revision of this comment put the line at PHYS_OFFSET +
+	 * 0x12310000. That address is the CPC -- the first *populated* block
+	 * in the register space rather than the start of it -- and bounding
+	 * at it admitted 35 MiB whose alias faults on the unpopulated holes
+	 * and lands in live registers elsewhere. The CBM pools at
 	 * 0x23800000..0x25200000 that seale_avm.dtsi reserves cannot move,
 	 * only be extended past.
 	 */
